# Result clarity

**Doc link:** <https://github.com/brando90/judges-dont-need-psychometrics-or-corr/blob/main/.cursor/rules/result-clarity.mdc>

**TLDR:** A reader must know, without opening code, which data a number was computed on, which metric it is, and what was subtracted from what.

Read `AGENTS.md`, section “Reporting results: name the data, the metric and the comparison.” It holds the same project rule as `CLAUDE.md`. In short:

1. **Data:** say train, validation or held-out test, what was held out and how many, and whether the held-out ratings were seen in earlier work. Label in-sample numbers as in-sample.
2. **Metric:** name, one-line definition, range or units, which direction is better, and a reference value for scale. Spell out acronyms at first use; always write “ranked probability score (RPS)”.
3. **Differences:** subtract the same metric on the same data for two methods. Name methods by how they were trained or chosen (“Pearson-trained judge”), write the order in words and say what a positive value means. Never write a bare “CE minus Pearson”.
4. **Uncertainty:** interval type, level, resampling unit and number of draws.
5. **How the judge was chosen:** search space, objective and its data, optimizer or search, start and budget, and whether the optimum is guaranteed; a one-line version goes in every caption showing fitted or selected judges.

Every plot axis label itself names its metric and split, never only the panel title; difference axes state their order. Data plots with numeric axes have gridlines (major, plus faint minor lines on log axes). Frozen result files are not rewritten; each experiment folder has a `HOW_TO_READ_RESULTS.md` instead.
